\chapter{Compiler modes and artifacts}
\label{ch:5}
\index{Compiler modes and artifacts}

Use the smallest compiler mode that can answer your question. \OnxCodeTerm{--tokens} investigates tokenization. \OnxCodeTerm{--emit-ast} emits the macro-expanded pre-PPX AST. \OnxCodeTerm{--deps} describes source loading. \OnxCodeTerm{--check} and \OnxCodeTerm{--types} are useful for source and type diagnostics. \OnxCodeTerm{--emit-c} exposes generated C11, while \OnxCodeTerm{--emit-ir}, \OnxCodeTerm{--ir-cfg}, and \OnxCodeTerm{--run-ir} inspect or run the supported MIR subset. Each output has its own consumers and assumptions.

\begin{OnxCodeBlock}[numbers=none,language={}]
build/on1x/on1xc --emit-c on1x/examples/hello.on1x -o build/hello.c
build/on1x/on1xc --emit-ir on1x/examples/mir.on1x -o build/example.mir
\end{OnxCodeBlock}

Emitting C and linking an executable are separate operations. The generated translation unit expects the runtime and, in some profiles, companion kernels. Use the helper in \cref{ch:4} to link a normal program. Use MIR modes when investigating a verified MIR feature. If MIR rejects a source program that direct C11 accepts, check the backend capability matrix before attempting to change the emitter. A smaller scalar example can isolate the behavior being inspected.

\section{Choose an observation for short circuiting}
The last print in the MIR example has a false left operand and a
division by zero inside its right operand. Listing tokens can establish
that the division sign is present. An AST can establish that the division
is nested in the expression on the right. Neither observation tells you
whether the compiled program will execute the division. That is a
control-flow question. Look for the path to the Boolean result that can
bypass the right operand, or for a simplified result that has eliminated
the untaken computation entirely.

Predict the output before running \OnxCodeTerm{--run-ir} on the example.
The loop visits zero through five; the factorial base case returns one
for each of the first two inputs. The six numeric lines are therefore
1, 1, 2, 6, 24 and 120. The assertion makes no line of its own. The last
line should be false, and the program should not terminate at the division.
This expectation is independent of the names and number of blocks in an
IR dump. Compare it with a direct C11 build of the same file if you are
investigating a backend change: the dumps need not look alike for the
two executions to agree on the observable behavior.

